Section 1.8 – Creating and Running Java Programs

1.8.1 What is the Java source filename extension, and what is the Java bytecode filename extension?

- Java source file extension: ".java"
- Java bytecode file extension: ".class"

1.8.2 What are the input and output of a Java compiler?

- Input: Java source code (".java" file).
- Output: Java bytecode (".class" file).

1.8.3 What is the command to compile a Java program?

The command is:

javac Test.java

1.8.4 What is the command to run a Java program?

The command is:

java Test

The ".class" extension is not included when running the program.

1.8.5 What is the JVM?

JVM stands for Java Virtual Machine.

It is a virtual machine that executes Java bytecode and allows Java programs to run on different platforms.

1.8.6 Can Java run on any machine? What is needed to run Java on a computer?

Java can run on different machines and operating systems.

A compatible Java Runtime Environment (JRE), or Java Development Kit (JDK), is needed to run Java programs.

1.8.7 If a NoClassDefFoundError occurs when you run a program, what is the cause of the error?

This error occurs when the JVM cannot find a class definition that was available when the program was compiled or loaded.

Possible causes include an incorrect classpath or a missing class file.

1.8.8 If a NoSuchMethodError occurs when you run a program, what is the cause of the error?

This error occurs when the JVM tries to call a method that cannot be found in the class at runtime.

It commonly happens when incompatible versions of classes or libraries are used.

