Section 1.10 – Programming Errors

1.10.1 What are syntax errors (compile errors), runtime errors, and logic errors?

- Syntax Errors: Errors caused by violating the grammar or rules of the programming language. They are detected during compilation.
- Runtime Errors: Errors that occur while a program is executing.
- Logic Errors: Errors that occur when a program runs successfully but produces an incorrect result.

1.10.2 Give examples of syntax errors, runtime errors, and logic errors.

1. Syntax Error:

System.out.println("Hello")

The semicolon is missing.

2. Runtime Error:

int x = 10 / 0;

This causes an "ArithmeticException" when executed.

3. Logic Error:

int length = 5;
int width = 3;
int area = 2 * (length + width);

This calculates the perimeter instead of the area.

1.10.3 If you forget to put a closing quotation mark on a string, what kind of error will be raised?

A syntax error (compile-time error) will be raised because the string literal is not properly terminated.

1.10.4 If your program needs to read integers, but the user entered strings, an error would occur when running this program. What kind of error is this?

It is a runtime error. For example, using "Scanner.nextInt()" when the user enters non-numeric text can cause an "InputMismatchException".

1.10.5 Suppose you write a program for computing the perimeter of a rectangle and you mistakenly write your program so it computes the area of a rectangle. What kind of error is this?

This is a logic error because the program executes successfully but produces the wrong result.

